  "client_view_text": " A write-ahead log is a key part of the ACID properties of modern databases.\nAn interesting question! A write-ahead log, also known as a transaction log, is a critical component of modern database systems that ensures data consistency and integrity. Its primary purpose is to record every modification made to the database before it is written to the actual database storage. This log is like a journal that keeps a record of all the changes, including updates, inserts, and deletes. By maintaining a write-ahead log, the database can recover from a crash or failure, ensuring that data remains consistent and accurate.\n\nHere's how it works: when a transaction is initiated in the database, the write-ahead log is updated with the details of the transaction before the actual change is made to the database storage. This way, even if the database crashes or fails during the middle of a transaction, the write-ahead log will still contain the details of the incomplete transaction. In the event of a crash or failure, the database can use the write-ahead log to recover the database to a consistent state. This is achieved by replaying the transactions from the log, effectively undoing any partial transactions and redoing the complete transactions. This ensures that the database returns to a consistent state, as if the crash or failure never"
}